  "subject": "Implementation brief: CATIO ZIP bundle support (CA-017 / #45) moves to TOP OF QUEUE by Director direction 2026-11-01, superseding the credentials-first sequencing in the 2026-11-01-0810 readiness report §6. First directed work item for the canonical-implementor seat, staffed earlier the same day (charter v2.3.0). The brief itself is published as GitHub tickets rather than carried in this memo, per Director direction that it be visible to the GTM advisory counterpart recorded in charter v2.2.0 — client half plus harness gap plus fixture at catdef-spec#45 (comment 6559523305), proxy half at catdef.org#8. This memo is the substrate pointer. Three substantive establishments: (1) the adopter's export is CONFORMANT, validated with this repo's own read_catio and validate_catdef — 43 items, 43 media resolved, zero Rule 2 violations, zero validator errors — so the non-conformant party is ours, not theirs; (2) this is a MUST violation of CATIO_SPEC line 240 failing on BOTH surfaces, whose plain consequence is that the reference renderer cannot render the reference catalog, since the canonical artifact is itself a bundle; (3) the conformance suite tests a PYTHON STAND-IN importer defined inside test_catio_bundle.py rather than the shipped JavaScript, which is the structural root cause of CA-017's 158-day survival — the renderer sits outside the standard that governs it, so it can fail a MUST indefinitely while CI stays green. Closing that harness gap is scoped INTO the work item, not deferred, because a fix without it preserves the blind spot that produced the delay. Recommended shape takes a simplification: /fetch stops decoding and passes bytes through, the client owns all sniff-and-unpack logic — one implementation instead of two, and file:// and proxy paths behave identically, which is the parity value #1 exists to protect. A validated 267 KB fixture with real photo bytes, its generator, and its validator are at S:/scratch/catdef-ca017-fixture/ and deliberately NOT committed, since conformance/fixtures/ is the implementor's lane and the existing convention builds archives at test time. Bounds restated not extended: push/deploy on catdef.org remains explicit per-act Director authorization, no normative spec text is needed or authorized, and the no-in-flight-amendment red line applies. The seat's own two findings (issue-key absent, GET /mcp 404) are real and stay queued behind this item under #41, not absorbed into #45.",

  "sent": "2026-11-01T09:52:10-05:00",
  "action_required": true,

  "body": "CA-017 / #45 moves to top of queue by Director direction 2026-11-01, superseding the credentials-first sequencing in the 2026-11-01-0810 readiness report §6. Assigned to canonical-implementor, staffed earlier the same day per charter v2.3.0; this is that seat's first directed work item. **The brief itself is on GitHub, not in this memo** — Director direction was to brief through a ticket so the work is visible to the GTM advisory counterpart recorded in charter v2.2.0. Client half, harness gap, and fixture: catdef-spec#45 comment 6559523305. Proxy half: catdef.org#8, a separate issue because it is a separate repository. The comment form was chosen over a new issue for the catdef-spec half deliberately: #45 is already the tracked item for this defect, and minting a second would reproduce the duplication this seat flagged in CA-017 itself. **Trigger for the priority change:** airtrov exports conformant CATIO bundles and cannot verify them against the reference renderer. An adopter blocked on the reference implementation is a different class of problem from an internally-observed defect, which is what CA-017 had been since 2026-05-26. **Three things the brief establishes.** First, the adopter's export is CONFORMANT — validated with this repository's own read_catio and validate_catdef: 43 items, 43 media resolved, zero Rule 2 violations, zero validator errors. The non-conformant party is ours. We should not ask them to change anything. Second, this is a MUST violation of CATIO_SPEC line 240, failing on both surfaces — /fetch returns 502 'Upstream body is not valid UTF-8' because it text-decodes, and the client loader accepts .opencatalog then throws in JSON.parse on PK bytes. Stated plainly: the reference renderer cannot render the reference catalog, because canonical/riverside-heritage-reference-v1.4.opencatalog is itself a bundle. Per value #4 — the conformance suite is the standard — a reference implementation failing a MUST is a conformance defect in the flagship, not a bug backlog item. Third, and most important: the conformance suite tests a PYTHON STAND-IN importer defined inside test_catio_bundle.py, not the shipped JavaScript. Nothing in the 127-test suite exercises the renderer. The suite proves the spec is implementable; it does not prove the reference implementation implements it. That is the structural reason CA-017 survived 158 days without tripping an alarm, and closing it is scoped INTO the work item rather than deferred, because a fix without it leaves the identical blind spot in place. **One simplification recommended:** do not implement ZIP handling twice. /fetch stops decoding and passes bytes through; the client owns all sniff-and-unpack logic, serving both the proxy path and local file selection. One implementation instead of two, and file:// and proxy paths then behave identically — the parity value #1 exists to protect. A corollary flagged for the implementor: if the loader is split out of the single HTML file, it must be a classic script tag rather than type=module, because ES modules are CORS-blocked over file:// and would break L1 in the subtlest way available — working when served, failing silently when opened as a file. **Fixture:** a validated 267 KB bundle with real photo bytes, plus its generator and validator, at S:/scratch/catdef-ca017-fixture/. Trimmed from the adopter's 6.2 MB export to 3 items and 3 photos while preserving mixed compression ({deflate: 1, stored: 3}) so both code paths are exercised. Deliberately NOT committed: conformance/fixtures/ is the implementor's lane and the existing convention builds archives at test time, so whether a binary belongs there is their call. One hazard recorded in the brief so a regeneration does not rediscover it — pruning the enumerated-value pools is load-bearing, since keeping all five while dropping 40 of 43 items leaves orphan values, trips the data.values superset rule, and yields an invalid fixture that would test the wrong thing. **Bounds restated, not extended:** push and deploy on catdef.org remain explicit per-act Director authorization, because CA-013 Call 2 leaves no staging step and commit is production there. No normative spec text is needed or authorized — CATIO already specifies this — and a gap met during implementation is filed as feedback, never edited in. Issue and PR creation stays blocked on the §2.1 attribution problem; commit attribution does not, per the e641be2 / 5d3e453 precedent, so git config should be set to the ratified canonical-implementor identity before the first commit — it currently points at catdef-maintainer, a seat holding no incumbent. **On the seat's own findings:** the absent catdef.org/mcp/issue-key route and the GET /mcp 404 are both real, both correctly attributed to that seat, and both belong to #41 rather than here. They stay queued behind this item; filing them was right, absorbing them into #45 would not be. The application's second patch finding is resolved rather than queued — gh reports catdef/catdef.org as PUBLIC, so renderer/README.md calling it private is the stale half. **Readiness-report blocker 4 is now closed** — it asked whether airtrov exports plain JSON or a bundle, because that decided whether #45 was launch-blocking. It was a bundle; #45 was launch-blocking. Blockers 1 through 3 remain open Director calls, and charter v2.2.0 and v2.3.0 remain uncommitted drafts awaiting ratification by merge.",
  "body_ref": "2026-11-01-0952--catdef-strategist--canonical-implementor--implementation-brief-ca017-catio-bundle-support.body.md",

